r in which the state was on  both sides of the argument possibly be  considered just it couldn't which is why  ancient Roman commentator Lucan called  Civil Wars wars which would bring no  triumphs arguably the first Civil War  started when a high-ranked Roman  politician with a grudge named sulla  marched his troops through the city  interestingly this seems to have  occurred on impulse Armitage says the  council seems to have had no plans to  March but his loyal soldiers  urged him on what marked this as the  first Civil War is that the soldiers  treated Rome like any outside territory  they were trying to conquer and employed  all the formal signs of War like  trumpets and flags nobody in the Senate  knew how to respond to this Invasion  nothing like this had ever happened  before Sola was Victorious and assumed a  role as dictator of Rome his enemies  sought Vengeance and a second Civil War  ensued just a few years later as  Armitage explains Sola had not intended  anything remotely resembling a military  takeover of the Republic and yet this  act had marked the beginning of a cycle  of civil violence one that would not end  until the creation of the Empire with  the elevation of augustus's emperor in  27 BCE as Armitage notes the phrase  Civil War was spoken sparing if ever at  all because of the harsh memories of  major conflicts the war is precipitated  by sulla's actions would come to define  a huge part of Roman history Rome's  experiences with Civil War as recounted  by many of the Empire's most famous  writers and thinkers would then go on to  influence the Latin West for centuries  stories of Rome's Civil War consumed  European intellectuals and artists alike  according to Armitage inspired some of  the most creative political thinking of  well into the enlightenment he writes  indeed for more than a millennium and a  half Civil War was viewed through Rome  tinted spectacles while it's hard to  definitively say that Rome's history  directly informed early modern European  history this pattern would play out  quite similarly as Europe endured  endless cycles of violence for centuries  European thinkers contextualized these  wars through their understanding of  ancient Rome's past and concluded that  as Armitage puts it civil war was one of  the distinguishing marks of civilization  that is to say you couldn't have a  modern liberal State without the  perennial threat of Civil War this was a  depressing pill to swallow and the  optimism of the Enlightenment era in  Europe which began in the late 17th  century and came to fruition in the 18th  led to some rebranding of Civil War by  intellectuals essentially they took  Civil War and repackaged it as  Revolution as German historian Reinhardt  kosaleck argues theories of Revolution  emerged as a concept in contrast to that  of Civil War Armitage adds that the  nascent category of Revolution was  designed in part to repress the memories  of Civil War and to replace them with  something more constructive more hopeful  and more forward-looking where Civil War  was senseless violence inflicted without  hope for anything better Revolution was  justifiable violence and acted with  hopes of creating a better world the  murkiness of the distinction between  civil war and revolution often plays out  in real time for example when it took  place many viewed the American  Revolution as a British Civil War or the  American Civil War not until a year into  the war to the South Carolina judge call  it the American Revolution and the  language wasn't adopted officially until  1770 9. similarly the American Civil War  was for decades called The War of  rebellion in the north which viewed the  South as rebelling against their shared  state in the South which viewed the  conflict as an international one between  two independent nations it was called  the war between the states not until  1907 was it officially dubbed a Civil  War by Congress thus from the very  Inception of modern Revolution the  distinction between rebellion and Civil  War has been difficult to parse as  Armitage writes it's easy to perform the  conjugation I am a revolutionary you are  a rebel they are engaged in a civil war  today Civil Wars have come to Define  most of the armed conflict in the world  since 1989 there's been an average of 29  Civil Wars going on at any given time  that's because after the fall of the  Soviet Union Civil Wars skyrocketed  reaching their highest numbers in modern  history until 2019 when they peaked  again so it doesn't seem all that  surprised amazing the people are worried  about impending war in America as civil  war scholar Barbara Walter explains if  you are an analyst in a foreign country  looking at events in America the same  way you look at events in Ukraine or the  Ivory Coast or Venezuela you would go  down a checklist assessing each of the  conditions that make Civil War likely  and what you would find is that the  United States a democracy founded more  than two centuries ago has entered very  dangerous territory so why the concern  well Walter is a member of the political  instability task force created by the  U.S government to assess which variables  make Civil War likely the task force  came up with 38 possible variables  ranging from poverty to corruption to  population size and did predictive  modeling to see which factors were most  likely to contribute to Interstate  conflict and to their surprise the best  predictors were not income equality or  poverty it was what they call the policy  score of the nation a policy score ranks  a country on a 21 point scale from  autocracy to democracy with plus 10  being full democracy and -10 being a  full autocracy countries like Norway New  Zealand Denmark and Canada are plus 10.  Saudi Arabia is a minus 10. in the  middle scoring between -5 and plus five  is what's known as a inocracy or a  partial democracy anocracies include  Algeria at a plus two and Egypt at a  minus four importantly anocracies are  the countries most likely to descend  into Civil War as Walter writes all the  things that experts thought should  matter in the outbreak of Civil War  somehow didn't it wasn't the poorest  countries that were at the highest risk  of conflict or the most unequal or the  most ethnically or religiously  heterogeneous or even the most  repressive it was living in a partial  democracy that made citizens more likely  to pick up a gun and begin to fight the  task force found that inocracies are two  times more likely to endure political  ability or Civil War than autocracies  and three times more likely than  democracies we might assume that super  oppressive autocracies are most likely  to Spur the mass anger necessary to  facilitate a civil war but since 1870  the world has experienced several waves  of massive democratization which have  been closely accompanied by waves of  Civil Wars Walter concludes that when a  nation moves from an autocracy to a  democracy this in-between transition  period is when it's at a high risk of  enduring Civil War she writes a primary  reason for revolt is that Democratic  transitions create new winners and  losers because the new government and  anenocracy is often fragile and the rule  of law is still developing the losers  former Elites opposition leaders  citizens who once enjoyed advantages are  not sure the administration will be fair  or that they will be protected this can  create genuine anxieties about the  future this is particularly true if the  country's attempt to transition to  democracy rapidly lie like some of the  ussr's former satellite States as  opposed to gradually like say Mexico but  what Walter is really alarmed by isn't  autocracies turning into democracies  which have constituted a large  percentage of civil wars in recent  memory she notes a more recent  phenomenon an increasing number of  democracies are becoming less and less  Democratic veering towards autocratic on  the quality scale such 