y many metrics ethnicity education  level religious affiliation and  geography specifically rural versus  Urban and for the most part these  parties seem to hate each other a study  by Scholars Nathan piccalmo and Lillian  Mason found that 20 percent of  Republicans and 15 percent of Democrats  think the country would be better off if  large percentages of the other parties  straight up died and this might lead us  to assuming that Civil War 2.0 is on the  horizon but Civil War in America today  would not look like the 19th century  Walter predicts that it would take the  form of insurgencies I.E revolts against  the government and preparations have  been made America saw their eyes a  far-right militia groups recruiting  members stockpiling arms and even  traveling abroad to train in the early  1990s but in recent years militia  activity has skyrocketed between 2008  and 2011 the number of militias in  America ballooned from 43 to 334.  importantly insurgencies typically  follow three predictable stages  according to the CIA the first is  pre-insurgency where groups build a  common identity centered around  grievances second comes incipient  conflict where random acts of violence  are committed the final stage is open  Insurgency which involves organized  attacks ranging from guerrilla warfare  to assassinations to bombings of  infrastructure like Bridges or schools  Walter argues that the January 6th  attack on the capital was the first Act  of open Insurgency in the United States  she writes we are a factionalized  anocracy that is quickly approaching the  open Insurgency stage which means we are  closer to Civil War than any of us would  like to believe and as we've seen in the  past this is concerning for a couple of  reasons which we can draw from history  one insurgents will likely view  themselves as revolutionaries and two  the launching of one Civil War can often  precipitate unavoidable cycles of future  Civil Wars and all these tensions are  magnified by social media Walter cites  social media's penchant for prioritizing  provocative content is having material  political effects  um we did a whole video about that so  check it out she notes Myanmar as a  prime example where Facebook promoted  content that fanned the Flames of a  horrific ethnic cleansing despite  persistent warnings from journalists and  human rights organizations that this was  happening Walter argues that social  media platforms pursuit of profit serves  to promote fascism all over the world  dividing Nations turning violent  impulses viral and eventually making  Civil War more likely with the United  States having the third biggest  population of social media users this  certainly seems like cause for concern  but there is another side to all of this  that's equally important as journalist  fintino tool cautions predicting future  Civil Wars can act actually make those  conflicts more likely to occur as it  increases paranoia on both sides so in  some ways predicting a future Civil War  can be a self-fulfilling prophecy what's  more it's important to remember no  democracy that is slid into anocracy has  experienced a full-on civil war yet  Walter is simply calling attention to an  alarming new phenomenon another reason  to be skeptical is that the United  States Army is For Better or Worse way  stronger than it was in the first Civil  War when it was composed of  approximately sixteen thousand members  spread across the country today we have  1.3 million soldiers 900 000 in reserve  and about 450 000 in the National Guard  absent a military coup this makes an  armed Insurrection significantly harder  to carry out furthermore America's  factions are less concentrated  geographically than they were in the  19th century with even the reddest  States containing liberal Urban centers  so it's hard to imagine War breaking out  between regions and short we're not  going to say America is on the brink of  Civil War and not just because that  makes such a thing more likely but none  of this answers the question why all the  interstate tensions well philosophers  have been thinking about Civil War by  various names ever since they started  considering the structure of society  which means at least as far back as  Plato's Republic Plato got the ball  rolling on philosophers being concerned  with the question of how to keep a state  Empire or political community in general  functioning and how to avoid violent  conflict within a polity and planning  his ideal Society he offer tips on  everything from education to child  rearing to should be in charge which was  a philosopher unfortunately he didn't  really account for things like natural  disasters and foreign invasions which  are both factors that eventually led to  the downfall of the Greek polity  eventually this fall got to Thomas Hobbs  who thought that what you really need is  a king who everyone obeys even if he  sucks because even the worst government  is better than the best Civil War  eventually Carl Schmidt a 20th century  political theorist and a literal Nazi  jumped into the chat the state's job for  Schmidt is to protect its citizens while  creating the conditions for any internal  issues to be solved with non-violent  political communication because the  opposite Civil War is the same as the  end of the state itself as scholar Jan  kujowski explains Civil War as Schmidt  puts it equals the dissolution of the  state as this is the end of a  territorially closed organized Unity  that is not available to political alien  entities instead of a closed system  where shared values enable political  exchange we get all out conflict so how  does this happen according to kujowski  the emergence of Civil War might be  related to any sort of situation in  which a political force would emerge  that would gain great popularity that  would be able to guarantee to society  more safety than official state  authorities in such a case the  submission nation of the citizens would  be directed at specifically this force  and not the weak state that would be  unable to provide safety put more simply  when the state seems incapable of  protecting its citizens but another  Force emerges that can or at least  appears to be then a civil war is born  people side with the new force in the  face of what appears to be an  increasingly illegitimate State conflict  ensues this is concerning whether or not  you think your state is doing a great  job of protecting you at the same time  Plato's dream of a long-term peace what  he called stasis is just that a dream  that's not viable in a world where we  can't control natural disasters and  economic turmoil and the idea of a  benevolent Monarch has a history of  going pretty poorly so we're left with  Schmidt's guidelines to the stakes of  and prerequisites for a civil war a  government without legitimacy met by  another force that offers a potentially  better scenario for at least some of the  state's citizens so the best way that  American government can avoid Civil War  is to attempt to gain back a greater  level of legitimacy which is a tricky  feat in such a polarized country and at  the end of the day it would be silly for  us to predict whether Civil War will  happen much less how to avoid it which  is maybe not the most satisfying  conclusion but did you really come to a  YouTube channel to get exact predictions  on the future of American political  stability but what do you all think a  Civil War in America starting to feel  inevitable how can such a conflict be  avoided and why do Civil War seem to be  the new Mainstay of armed conflict in  the world please let us know what you  think in the comments thanks as always  to our patrons please check out our  patreon page if you haven't before it is  the single best way that you can  directly support the channel and the  work that we're doing as always thank  you so much for watching we'll see you  later  [Music]  thank you  [Music]
 

